WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440
We now combine two
vanishing sums into one.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.180
Let u be an odd integer greater than one,

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.620
and let I contain its
distinct prime divisors.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.140
A subset S selects roots
whose product is zeta sub S.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.980
The empty subset gives the input
one. Work in the common ring A,

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:22.620
with i, those roots, and one
over u adjoined to the integers.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:29.560
Write c sub S for c evaluated at zeta
sub S, and similarly for d and g.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.900
Node seven point three
gives two congruences.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:36.660
The alternating sum of
d sub S minus c sub S,

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:41.140
divided exactly by two and then
by c sub S, has residue zero.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.500
So does the alternating sum
of g sub S minus c sub S,

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:50.040
divided exactly by one
plus i and then by c sub S.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:55.820
Both reductions take place modulo one
plus i, in the same quotient ring.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.360
Match the subset indices in the two
rows. We can add corresponding terms.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.500
Recall the polynomial E of P.

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:09.160
It is d of P minus c of P, divided by two,

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.500
plus g of P minus c of
P, divided by one plus i.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:19.780
These are exact coefficientwise
divisions, performed before reduction.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:24.500
At each input, c sub S is a unit in A.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.440
Factor its inverse out of
the two terms in one column.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.180
The bracketed numerator is
precisely E evaluated at zeta sub S.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:39.420
Their sum is therefore E at zeta
sub S, divided by c at zeta sub S.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.020
This identity is exact in A. Make
that replacement for every subset.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:50.520
Adding the two zero residues gives one
alternating sum, which we call script S.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:55.380
It is the sum over all subsets of
I of minus one to the size of S,

00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:00.500
times E at zeta sub S
divided by c at zeta sub S.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:04.780
Its residue modulo one plus i is zero.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.640
The signs remember the even and
odd products from which we started.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:10.580
In characteristic two,

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:15.000
plus and minus agree, but the signs still
record the exact sum we constructed.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:20.960
For the guiding case u equals three,
there are just two inputs, one and omega.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.560
Here the common rotation gives c at
omega equal to lambda times c at one.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:31.600
The fixed linear combination
defining E gives E at omega equal to

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.500
lambda times E at one. In the
normalized ratio, lambda cancels.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.980
We divide only by the nonzero
denominator c, so this remains valid

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:45.000
even when E at one is zero. The
two normalized values are equal,

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.320
and their difference,
script S, is zero exactly.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:54.340
For general odd u greater than one,
the product argument gives residue zero

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:58.640
without requiring one common
rotation across every input.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:03.440
Node eight will apply the coefficient
congruence, E of P congruent to the

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.140
all-ones polynomial J of P modulo
one plus i, to this full subset sum.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.140
That separate calculation
gives residue one,

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:14.320
incompatible with the zero
residue established here.
